From 4d240c493899c13003cb22c1adc22aeb0001e08a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Sergey Fedorov <barracuda@macos-powerpc.org>
Date: Thu, 5 Nov 2026 20:37:27 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 433/433] trans: MRUSTC_CODEGEN_UNIT_SIZE splits the C output
 into translation units

cargo 0.99.0 (cargo-c 0.10.25): "virtual memory exhausted: Cannot allocate
memory" from the C compiler on the crate's 750MB libcargo-0_99_0_H2.rlib.c.
GCC parses a translation unit completely before compiling it, and a 32-bit
GCC (ppc on 10.6) runs out of address space on a source that size.

With MRUSTC_CODEGEN_UNIT_SIZE=<MiB> (GCC mode only; unset or 0 = as before)
the C backend records where the shared declarations end (types, constructors,
prototypes, static declarations), where the static definitions end and where
each function body ends, and after the single source is complete writes
`<out>.<n>.c` units of about that many bytes of bodies: every unit starts
with the declarations, unit 0 also has the static definitions, the last one
the trailer (global asm, an executable's shims and main). Each unit compiles
with the single compile's compiler and options, `cc -nostdlib -r` merges the
objects into the object the single compile would have produced (the GCC
Darwin and Linux link specs pass `-r` through without start files or default
libraries), the unit files are removed; an executable or dynamic library
links the merged object. The single `.c` stays, for reference. Nothing is
split when the bodies fit one unit.

What the single compile emitted as `static` must be shared by the units:
this crate's copies of other crates' generic/inlined functions and statics
(every crate that uses one emits it under the same mangled name) and
extern-weak static shims. With units enabled, such a function or static is
a hidden-visibility symbol renamed per crate (`#define <mangled>
<mangled>__<crate>` next to its prototype, before any use), every static is
declared `extern` and defined in unit 0 (a zero static too, which the single
compile left as a tentative definition), and the extern-weak shim (a pointer)
is `static` in each unit. Items with a link name keep `static`.

Assisted-by: Claude Fable 5.1
